# /dev/ohc — this board's IO under the names on the case.
#
# Every kernel path underneath is a number that is an accident of probe order:
# /dev/lircN is a first-free minor from a counter shared by every rc driver in
# the kernel, and ttyS numbering depends on CONFIG_SERIAL_8250_RUNTIME_UARTS —
# the very setting whose mainline default silently drops ttyS4 on an HC-800.
# The names do not move.
#
#   /dev/ohc/ir/out1 … outN     rear IR jacks, as labelled
#   /dev/ohc/ir/front-blaster   internal emitter behind the front panel
#   /dev/ohc/ir/front-receiver  the learner on the same panel
#   /dev/ohc/gpiochip           the chip carrying the relays and contacts
#   /dev/ohc/serial/1 … N       rear RS-232, in the order board.env declares
#   /dev/ohc/radio/zigbee       the EmberZNet NCP, wherever it is wired
#   /dev/ohc/radio/zwave        likewise
#
# RELAYS AND CONTACTS GET NO ENTRY OF THEIR OWN, and that is not a gap we could
# close: Linux has no per-line device node. A line is addressed by offset inside
# a chip through an ioctl, so /dev/ohc/relay1 could only be a symlink to the
# whole chip — which every other relay would point at too, and opening it would
# not give you relay 1. That is a lie, not a convenience.
#
# What a line has instead is a NAME, which is strictly better, and the chip that
# carries them does get an entry because its number is the thing that moves:
#
#   gpioinfo /dev/ohc/gpiochip     # lists relay1..N, contact1..N
#   gpioset $(gpiofind relay1)=1
#   gpioget $(gpiofind contact4)
#
# The naming rule itself is in ohc-udev-name, not duplicated here: the serial
# order comes from board.env and the IR labels come from the driver's DEV_NAME,
# and neither belongs in a match expression.

# IR. DEV_NAME lives on the parent rc device, not on the lirc node, so this
# cannot be an ENV match — the helper reads it out of sysfs.
SUBSYSTEM=="lirc", PROGRAM="/usr/bin/ohc-udev-name lirc %k", SYMLINK+="ohc/ir/%c"

# Host UARTs. Nothing matches on the EA family, where the user ports are routed
# through the microcontroller and have no device node at all — the same rule
# will name them the day those become real ttys.
#
# Not KERNEL=="ttyS*": the CA-1's are ttymxc*, and which 8250-or-not the SoC
# fits is exactly the kind of thing board.env already answers.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", PROGRAM="/usr/bin/ohc-udev-name serial %k", SYMLINK+="ohc/serial/%c"

# Radios. Same helper, same board.env shape; the kind IS the name because a
# board has at most one of each.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", PROGRAM="/usr/bin/ohc-udev-name radio %k", SYMLINK+="ohc/radio/%c"

# The gpiochip carrying the relays and contacts.
SUBSYSTEM=="gpio", KERNEL=="gpiochip*", PROGRAM="/usr/bin/ohc-udev-name gpio %k", SYMLINK+="ohc/%c"
